  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "K.M.Vinod vs Food Inspector on 13 October, 2020 In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present The Honourable Mr.Justice N.Anil Kumar Tuesday, The 13Th Day Of October 2020 / 21St Aswina, 1942 Crl.Rev.Pet.No.179 Of 2005(B) Against The Judgment In Cra 118/2003 Dated 09-11-2004 Of Additional Sessions Court, Thodupuzha St 498/2000 Dated 07-04-2003 Of Judicial First Class Magistrate Court, Idukki Revision PETITIONER/APPELLANT/3rd Accused: K.M.Vinod, S/O.Madhavan, Kooralikotta House, Methal.P.O., Perumbavoor, Ernakulam. By Advs. Sri.Santhosh Subramanian Sri.Sherry J. Thomas Sri.S.Sreekumar(North Paravur) Sri.E.A.Thankappan Respondents/Complainant/State: 1 Food Inspector, Thodupuzha Circle, Thodupuzha. 2 State Of Kerala, Rep. By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. By Senior Public Prosecutor Sri.M.S.Breez This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 13.10.2020, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Crl.R.P.No.179 of 2005 ..2.. K.M.Vinod vs Food Inspector on 13 October, 2020 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/75280521/ 1 Order",
    "facts": "Dated this the 13th day of October, 2020 This criminal revision petition is directed against the judgment dated 09.11.2004 in Crl.A.No.118/2003 on the file of the Additional Sessions Court, Thodupuzha. The revision petitioner was the 3rd accused in S.T.No.498/2000 on the file of the Judicial First Class Magistrate Court, Idukki and the appellant in Crl.A.No.118/2003. 2. The original complaint was filed by the Food Inspector, Thodupuzha Circle against A1 to A4 alleging commission of offences punishable under Sections 2(ia) (m), 7(i) and 16(1)(a)(i) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (hereinafter referred to as 'the PFA Act') and the Rules framed thereunder. 3. The case of the complainant is that on 23.11.1999, he had purchased three bottles of pooja ghee from the bakery of the 1st accused situated at Vazhathopu ..3.. Grama Panchayat which is within his jurisdiction in accordance with law. After complying with requisite formalities, three bottles of pooja ghee were sealed in the presence of the 1st accused. Thereafter, the 1st accused produced the purchase bill to prove that he had purchased the said food item from the 2 nd accused. A part of the sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. On 17.1.2000, the Public Analyst forwarded Form-III report through the Local Health Authority stating that the sample of ghee sent for analysis was adulterated. The 3 rd accused is the nominee of the manufacturer. 4. During the proceedings, the 3rd accused exercised his right to get the second sample analysed through the Central Food Laboratory. On analysis the Central Food Laboratory sent a report stating that the sample of ghee sent for analysis was adulterated as well as misbranded. .. 4.. 5. On the appearance of the accused 1 to 4 before the court, the 2nd accused filed an application to implead the 5th accused on the allegation that he had purchased the said ghee from the 5th accused. The said application was allowed and the 5th accused was impleaded. 6. In the meanwhile, the complainant filed an application to amend the charge and incorporate Section 2 (ix)(k). In view of the CFL report the said application was allowed and an offence of selling misbranded food article was also added to the charge. 7. During the trial, PWs.1 to 3 were examined and marked Exts.P1 to P26 on the side of the complainant. DWs.1 and 2 were examined and marked Exts.D1 to D3 on the side of the accused. K.M.Vinod vs Food Inspector on 13 October, 2020 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/75280521/ 2 8. 10. Heard both sides. 11. The prosecution was initiated based on Form-III report received from the Public Analyst. Form-II report also confirmed the finding contained in the Form-III report. However, it is stated that the food item purchased was misbranded as well.",
    "arguments": "In Pepsico India Holdings ..7.. Pvt.Ltd. v. Food Inspector and another (2010 (4) KHC 767), the Apex Court held that the laboratories where sample of articles of food for adulteration may be analysed by the Public Analyst in accordance with Section 23(1A) (ee) and 23(1A)(hh) of the PFA Act, 1954.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "In Pepsico India Holdings ..7.. Pvt.Ltd. v. Food Inspector and another (2010 (4) KHC 767), the Apex Court held that the laboratories where sample of articles of food for adulteration may be analysed by the Public Analyst in accordance with Section 23(1A) (ee) and 23(1A)(hh) of the PFA Act, 1954."
  },
